<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TDataSize</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TDataSize" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TDataSize</tt> displays a byte count in the unit a reader expects. The <tt>Size</tt>
property holds the count in bytes, and the control picks the largest unit that leaves a
number above one.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TDataSize Size="475837458" UseMarketingSize="true" Abbreviate="true" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
That tag renders <tt>476 MB</tt>. The control extends <tt>TLabel</tt> and renders a
<tt>&lt;span&gt;</tt>, so <tt>CssClass</tt> and <tt>Style</tt> apply. The control replaces
the label's <tt>Text</tt> with the formatted size.
</p>

<h2>Properties</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Size</tt></td>
	<td>0</td>
	<td>The number of bytes to display. A negative value raises a <tt>TInvalidDataValueException</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>UseMarketingSize</tt></td>
	<td>true</td>
	<td>True divides by 1000 and names decimal units. False divides by 1024 and names binary units.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Abbreviate</tt></td>
	<td>true</td>
	<td>True writes the unit symbol. False writes the unit name, singular or plural to match the number.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Units</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The two boolean properties combine into four outputs. The table shows each one for a
<tt>Size</tt> of 1500000 bytes under the <tt>en</tt> culture.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>UseMarketingSize</th><th>Abbreviate</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td>true</td><td>true</td><td><tt>1.5 MB</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>true</td><td>false</td><td><tt>1.5 megabytes</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>false</td><td>true</td><td><tt>1.43 MiB</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>false</td><td>false</td><td><tt>1.43 mebibytes</tt></td></tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The decimal units run B, KB, MB, GB, TB, PB, EB, ZB, YB, RB, and QB. The binary units run
B, KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB, PiB, EiB, ZiB, YiB, RiB, and QiB. The spelled-out names run byte,
kilobyte, megabyte, and so on for the decimal scale, and byte, kibibyte, mebibyte, and so
on for the binary scale. A size above the largest unit stays in that unit.
</p>

<h2>Localization</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The unit symbols and names pass through <tt>Prado::localize()</tt>, so a translated
application supplies its own wording. The number itself follows the culture of the control.
The <tt>Culture</tt> property overrides the application culture for one control, and the
<tt>Charset</tt> property sets the character set of the output.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TDataSize Size="1500000" Culture="de_DE" Abbreviate="false" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

